      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Consider the two-digit integer $n$ as $ab$, which represents the digits $a$ and $b$. In this case, $n = 10a + b$, and switching the digits gives $10b + a$. According to the problem, switching the digits and adding 3 results in $2n$. Hence, we set up the equation $(10b + a) + 3 = 2(10a + b) = 20a + 2b$ and solve for $a$ and $b$. **Begin Solution**\n\n1. From the equation given, we have:\n    $(10b + a) + 3 = 2(10a + b)$.\n    \n2. Expanding and simplifying, we get:\n    $10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b \\implies 10b - 2b + a - 20a = -3 \\implies 8b - 19a = -3$.\n    \n3. In the equation above, the only integer solutions for $b$ and $a$ are:\n    - $a = 1$, $b = 2$.\n    - $a = 9$, $b = 9+9 = 18$ (This doesn't fit within a two-digit number constraint).\n\n  \nSo, the smallest two-digit number where the statement holds is $\\boxed{12}$.",
